=pod

=encoding UTF-8

=head1 NAME

Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::VGPU - Which PCI subsystem IDs mark an NVIDIA vGPU guest device

=head1 VERSION

version 0.002

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  use Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::VGPU;

  my $type = Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::VGPU->type_for('2236', '14b9', '10de');
  say $type // 'not a vGPU';   # NVIDIA A10-2Q

=head1 DESCRIPTION

A guest VM on an NVIDIA vGPU (Azure NVadsA10 v5, AWS G6f, a VMware / KVM
vGPU host) sees a PCI device with NVIDIA's vendor ID and the B<physical>
GPU's device ID -- C<lspci -nn> cannot tell it from the passed-through card.
The subsystem ID can: NVIDIA assigns every vGPU type its own, and publishes
the pairs in the open GPU kernel modules. Such a guest needs NVIDIA's
licensed vGPU guest (GRID) driver; the open C<nvidia.ko> of the distribution
and datacenter packages refuses an Ampere-or-newer vGPU function. See
L<Rex::GPU::Detect/NVIDIA vGPU guests> for how detection uses this and
L<Rex::GPU::NVIDIA/install_driver> for what it changes.

The pair comparison only knows the types in L</types>: a vGPU type NVIDIA
adds after the tag in L</source> is reported as a physical card until the
table is regenerated (C<maint/gen-vgpu-types.pl --source
path/to/open-gpu-kernel-modules>).

=head2 type_for

  my $type = Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::VGPU->type_for($device_id, $subsystem_id, $subsystem_vendor_id);
  # 'GRID A100X-1-5C' for ('20b0', '146f', '10de'); undef for a physical card

The vGPU type name for a PCI device as the guest sees it: the NVIDIA device
ID (the C<XXXX> of C<[10de:XXXX]>) and the subsystem ID (the C<[xxxx]> of
C<lspci -vmmnn>'s C<SDevice:> line), both four hex digits in any case. An
NVIDIA vGPU hands the guest the physical GPU's device ID and a subsystem ID
of its own per vGPU type, from NVIDIA's own table (L</types>). C<undef> when
the pair is not in the table -- a physical card, a passed-through one, or a
vGPU type newer than the table -- and for a missing or malformed ID. The
optional C<$subsystem_vendor_id> must be C<10de> when given: every row of
NVIDIA's table is, and an OEM board (subsystem vendor Dell, HPE, ...) whose
subsystem ID happens to equal one is not a vGPU. Called on a subclass, it
uses that subclass's L</types>.

=head2 types

  my %types = $class->types;   # '20b0:146f' => 'GRID A100X-1-5C', ...

The table: a flat list of pairs, the key C<dddd:ssss> (device ID and
subsystem ID, lowercase hex), the value the vGPU type name NVIDIA gives it.
Generated from C<sVgpuUsmTypes[]> in NVIDIA's open-gpu-kernel-modules
(C<src/common/shared/inc/g_vgpu_chip_flags.h>) by
C<maint/gen-vgpu-types.pl>; L</source> names the tag it was read from.
Turing to Blackwell Ultra.

Override it in a subclass to add a type this release does not know yet; a
later pair wins over an earlier one with the same key:

  package My::GPU::VGPU;
  use parent 'Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::VGPU';

  sub types {
    my ( $self ) = @_;
    return ( $self->SUPER::types, '2bb5:9999' => 'NVIDIA RTXPro6000-99Q' );
  }

L<Rex::GPU::Detect> reads the built-in table, not a subclass (as it does for
L<Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::Requirement/generations>).

=head2 source

  my $src = Rex::GPU::NVIDIA::VGPU->source;
  # { file => 'src/common/shared/inc/g_vgpu_chip_flags.h', tag => '615.71.09',
  #   generated => '2026-09-24', entries => 1135 }

Where L</types> came from: the file in NVIDIA's open-gpu-kernel-modules, its
tag, the date the table was generated and the number of pairs.

=head1 LICENSE OF THE TABLE

The pairs in L</types> are taken from NVIDIA's open-gpu-kernel-modules, which
NVIDIA licenses as MIT (C<COPYING> there); the notice is reproduced in a
comment above L</types> in this file.

